    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Phishing for Information — Infrastructure Staging", "technique": "T1583.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-09-14 09:12:07 CEST, the internal recursive resolver 10.108.57.1 logged a burst of outbound DNS A-record lookups for a tight cluster of domains that are typo/homograph variants of the corporate apex domain corp.local: corp.io, corp.co, corp.net, corp.biz, corrp.local, corp-local.net, corp.local.com, c0rp.local, the Cyrillic-homograph corp.local (xn--cop-9cd.local punycode), corpo.local, corpe.local, corpz.local, corp-mail.local, corp-vpn.local, corp-portal.local. The query source recorded by Suricata DNS logging on the resolver path was data.srcip=45.83.12.7. Each lookup was spaced ~3 seconds apart — a deliberate, scripted enumeration cadence (not a human browser, not a bulk zone transfer). This is the classic Phishing-for-Information infrastructure-staging step: the actor is checking which lookalike domains already resolve (i.e. are registered and live) so they can select the most convincing one to host a credential-capture portal or send spoofed vendor email from.", "chain_reason": "Suricata DNS logging on the 10.108.57.1 resolver path fired rule.description matching 'DNS query to lookalike / typosquat domain of protected apex' with rule.groups containing 'suricata' + 'dns' for >12 distinct data.dns.rrname values all within Levenshtein distance <=2 of corp.local, all from a single data.srcip=45.83.12.7 inside a 30-minute window. The Wazuh correlation rule attached MITRE T1583.001 (Acquire Infrastructure: Domains) because the pattern is domain-selection reconnaissance.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T09:12:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Phishing for Information — Phishing Infrastructure Validation", "technique": "T1598", "what_happened": "At 09:27:30 CEST (~15-minute pause after the A-record sweep ended at ~09:13), the same data.srcip=45.83.12.7 issued a second, narrower wave of DNS lookups against the SAME resolver — this time MX-record (data.dns.rrtype=MX) queries against the first 6 lookalike domains only (corp.io, corp.co, corp.net, corp.biz, corrp.local, corp-local.net). MX-record probing on a typosquat domain has exactly one purpose in this context: confirm the lookalike domain can RECEIVE and SEND email — i.e. validate it as functional phishing infrastructure for a Spearphishing Service / Spearphishing Link campaign. The narrowing from 15 domains (Phase 1) to 6 domains (Phase 2) is the operator-selection signature: the actor filtered the A-record results and is now validating only the candidates worth weaponising.", "chain_reason": "Suricata DNS logging fired rule.description matching 'DNS MX-record query for lookalike domain' with data.dns.rrtype=MX and data.srcip=45.83.12.7 against a subset of the Phase-1 data.dns.rrname values. The 15-minute inter-phase pause + the domain-list narrowing is the human-operator review signature consistent with Phishing-for-Information infrastructure preparation (MITRE T1598). The correlation rule attached T1598 (Phishing for Information) because MX validation of typosquat domains is the canonical pre-send step of an info-harvesting phishing campaign.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T09:27:30+0200"}
